# Work Kernel (HARD RULE)

Coordination happens through the repo, never through chat memory. Chat dies with
the session; the ledger survives agents, machines, and developers. If the
machinery below does not exist yet in a repo, CREATE it on first use. Never skip
a step because the file is absent.

## The ledger
Tasks live in `.agents/tasks/<id>.yaml` (one file per task). Required fields:

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| id | short unique id, e.g. `T-0042` |
| title | one line |
| intent | the actual ask, 1-2 lines, verbatim where possible |
| partition | the OWNERSHIP.yaml partition this task writes to (one only) |
| depends_on | task ids that must be `landed` first |
| risk | low / medium / high / irreversible |
| acceptance | list of criteria, executable tests where possible |
| state | see state machine |
| claimant | agent identity, empty if unclaimed |
| lease_expires | ISO 8601 UTC, empty if unclaimed |
| evidence | links to artifacts once verifying/landed |

## State machine (only these transitions)
`draft -> ready -> claimed -> in-progress -> verifying -> queued -> landed`
Failure exits: `-> rejected` (keep the file, record why) or `-> blocked`
(name the blocker task or resource). Rejected work is never merged then
reverted; it is discarded and the task re-planned.

A task is not `ready` until acceptance criteria exist. A task with vague
acceptance criteria is a defect in the task, not a license to improvise.

## Leases (no work without a claim)
- To claim: write `claimant` + `lease_expires` (default now + 30 min).
- Heartbeat: refresh `lease_expires` every turn while working.
- Expired lease: only the supervisor releases it and reassigns. Never steal a
  live lease. An agent death loses minutes, not work, because state is on disk.
- One claimant per task. One task per writer agent at a time.

## Pull, not push
Writers pull the highest-priority `ready` task whose dependencies are `landed`
and whose partition is unheld. Priority order:
1. Restore a broken dev env or red trunk (always first).
2. Unblockers: tasks whose landing frees other partitions or agents.
3. Risk-adjusted value: user-facing correctness before internals.
4. Cosmetic, docs, cleanup.

## Supervisor role
The parent agent creates and splits tasks, watches leases, reassigns expired
ones, runs the merge queue, and reads the event log before planning. The
supervisor of a wave does NOT write code in that wave and does NOT audit its
own decomposition; a separate agent reviews the split.

## Idempotent and resumable
Every step must be safely re-runnable. Write state to the ledger each turn.
Any agent must be able to resume any task from the ledger plus the repo alone,
with zero chat context.

## Fast path (anti-bureaucracy valve)
A single trivial task (docs, one-file low-risk fix) may skip wave machinery
and run as one writer. It NEVER skips the verification gate (03) or the
handoff entry (04).

## Wave budget
Every wave states a token/time budget in its plan line and stops at budget to
reassess. Budget exhaustion is a normal stop, not a failure.

## Bootstrap
On first use in a repo: create `.agents/tasks/`, `.agents/leases/`,
`.agents/events.jsonl`, and `OWNERSHIP.yaml` (see 02). Commit them. These are
part of the repo, not local scratch.
